 [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "18 – 22 August 2008 — 15:30"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/819"} "Shunyi Aolinpike Shuishang Gongyuan, Mapo, Shunyi"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/258754"} "26 from 26 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Unlike the K-1 500, results for the K-1 1000 had been more consistent since the 2004 Olympics, with defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/90226"} "Eirik Verås Larsen"] " of Norway having taken the 2005 and 2007 European and and 2005 World Championships, while also coming in third at the 2007 Worlds. Great Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/90177"} "Tim Brabants"] ", meanwhile, was the 2006 and 2008 European and 2007 World Champion, and was runner-up at the 2007 Europeans and 2006 Worlds. The only other person to have captured a major international title was " [:a {:href "/athletes/10796"} "Markus Oscarsson"] " of Sweden, the 2006 World Champion and European bronze medalist. " [:a {:href "/athletes/105197"} "Ben Fouhy"] " of Australia and " [:a {:href "/athletes/105202"} "Adam Van Koeverden"] " of Canada, the 2004 Olympic silver and bronze medalists respectively, had also seen some success, with Fouhy having been the 2006 World bronze medalist and Van Koeverden the 2007 World runner-up."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Brabants, Van Koeverden, and Larsen all won their opening heats and advanced directly to the final, while Oscarsson and Fouhy, third-place in Brabants and Van Koeverden’s heats respectively, were sent to the semi-finals. Fouhy and Oscarsson were second and third respectively in a heat won by Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/116559"} "Ken Wallace"] ", a relatively unknown canoeist who would go on to win the K-1 500. Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/116560"} "Max Hoff"] ", the 2008 European bronze medalist, took the other heat. In the medal round, Brabants took command and won the gold medal by a margin of over a second; he would add to his collection the following day by taking bronze in the K-1 500. Wallace and Larsen, meanwhile, were neck and neck for the silver medal, with the latter coming out ahead by 0.142 seconds."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "99"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/90177"} "Tim Brabants"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "3:27.828 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:26.323 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "172"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/90226"} "Eirik Verås Larsen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "3:29.043 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:27.342 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "12"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/116559"} "Ken Wallace"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "3:30.306 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "3:33.255 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:27.485 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "175"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/105197"} "Ben Fouhy"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NZL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle"